## Shared Ownership with Rc

`Rc[T]` (Reference Counted) enables shared ownership of a value within a single thread. Multiple `Rc` handles point to the same data, which is freed when the last handle is dropped.

### Creating and Cloning Rc

```xiom
let shared = Rc.new(Vec[Int]());  // initial reference count = 1
shared.push(1);                    // access through Rc

let clone1 = shared.clone();      // ref count = 2
let clone2 = shared.clone();      // ref count = 3

// All three point to the same Vec
clone1.push(2);  // visible through shared and clone2 too
```

### Rc is Read-Only

Rc provides shared read access. To mutate, combine with `Cell` or `RefCell`:

```xiom
let rc = Rc.new(42);
// *rc = 43;  // cannot mutate directly through Rc
```

### Reference Count Tracking

```xiom
let rc = Rc.new(42);
let count = rc.ref_count();  // 1

let rc2 = rc.clone();
let count2 = rc.ref_count();  // 2

{
  let rc3 = rc.clone();
  let count3 = rc.ref_count();  // 3
}  // rc3 dropped — ref count back to 2
```

### When Rc is Dropped

When the last Rc handle is dropped, the inner value is also dropped:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let rc = Rc.new(Vec[Int]());
  rc.push(42);
  let clone = rc.clone();
  // Both rc and clone alive
  mem.drop(rc);        // ref count: 2 -> 1, data still alive
  mem.drop(clone);     // ref count: 1 -> 0, data dropped
  return 0;
}
```

### Use Cases

- Shared data in tree/graph structures
- Multiple owners for the same configuration
- Cached data shared across components

